Meridian's whole corpus is 1,454 chunks of 1536 numbers (8.9 MB).

      chunks   index size     search    per query
       1,000         6 MB      0.2 ms        0.2 ms
      10,000        61 MB      2.7 ms        2.7 ms
     100,000       614 MB     31.4 ms       31.4 ms
     500,000      3072 MB    503.5 ms      503.5 ms

Two things grow together, and the second one bites first.

In theory the time is linear: every query touches every vector, so ten times
the corpus is ten times the work.

In practice it is worse than linear. The last two rows are 5x
more data and 16.1x more time, because 3 gigabytes
stops fitting in the caches the processor is fast at reaching.

Where that happens is a property of your hardware, not of your corpus. Run this
listing on your own machine and you may get different numbers — including, on a
smaller one, a MemoryError rather than a slow answer. Brute force degrades
gracefully right up until it stops working.

Brute force is exact. That is worth something, and it is why the crossover is
further out than people assume: at ten thousand chunks it is instant, and a
great many production systems are smaller than that.
